        "problem": "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$",
        "worked_solution": "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0},\\,2\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is $⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.$",
