        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve",
        "problem": "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$",
        "worked_solution": "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
